<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Griquatown</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Klaarwater</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Griquatown is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cape">Northern Cape</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>. It began as Klaarwater, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> station founded in the early 1800s among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a>, and was renamed in 1813, when the missionary John Campbell helped the community adopt a written constitution. It was the early centre of the Griqua under the Kok and Waterboer families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/griquatown.jpg" alt="Griquatown" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Etienne Marais · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griekwastad_road.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
